WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.605
A flagpole stands on the ground and carries nothing but its own weight.

00:00:04.605 --> 00:00:06.226
How much does it shorten?

00:00:06.226 --> 00:00:08.951
And where inside it is the stress highest?

00:00:08.951 --> 00:00:11.480
Let's answer both with finite elements.

00:00:11.813 --> 00:00:14.477
This example is from the course slides.

00:00:14.477 --> 00:00:17.415
The column has height L, fixed at its base.

00:00:17.415 --> 00:00:19.533
Measure x upward from the base.

00:00:19.867 --> 00:00:24.055
Its weight acts all along it, pointing down.

00:00:24.055 --> 00:00:31.766
Per unit length that's rho, times A, times g: density, times area, times gravity.

00:00:31.766 --> 00:00:37.858
Since x points up, the load is negative: f equals minus rho A g.

00:00:37.858 --> 00:00:42.427
In our element formulas, c equals minus rho A g.

00:00:42.833 --> 00:00:47.145
Use three equal elements, each of length l equals L over three.

00:00:47.145 --> 00:00:50.157
Nodes one to four, from the base to the top.

00:00:50.157 --> 00:00:52.073
Node one sits on the ground.

00:00:53.066 --> 00:00:55.512
First, connect this back to the bowl.

00:00:55.512 --> 00:01:02.058
The column's Pi is the sum of every element's strain energy, minus the work potential of the loads.

00:01:02.058 --> 00:01:09.066
Take a partial derivative for each node and set it to zero, just as we did for the springs in episode two.

00:01:09.366 --> 00:01:15.648
Each element's energy is the spring formula, with stiffness E A over l.

00:01:15.648 --> 00:01:27.326
So the result is the same kind of matrix we built from springs: a stiffness matrix K, times the displacements q, equals the loads F.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.907
Element one connects nodes one and two.

00:01:30.907 --> 00:01:40.320
Its local spot one-one goes to the big matrix's spot one-one, and its block fills rows and columns one and two.

00:01:40.653 --> 00:01:43.270
Element two connects nodes two and three.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.165
Its local spot one-one goes to the big matrix's spot two-two.

00:01:47.165 --> 00:01:50.293
But spot two-two is already taken by element one.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.532
This is the key step of assembly, so watch it closely.

00:01:54.532 --> 00:01:58.733
Where two blocks land on the same spot, their entries add.

00:01:58.733 --> 00:02:00.400
One plus one gives two.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:00.689
Why?

00:02:00.689 --> 00:02:09.020
Because q two appears in two elements' energies, so the derivative with respect to q two picks up a term from each.

00:02:09.387 --> 00:02:16.570
Element three goes into rows and columns three and four, adding at spot three-three.

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:20.075
Everywhere no element touches, it's zero.

00:02:20.075 --> 00:02:32.987
The result: E A over l, times one, minus one, zero, zero; minus one, two, minus one, zero; zero, minus one, two, minus one; zero, zero, minus one, one.

00:02:35.099 --> 00:02:37.283
The loads assemble the same way.

00:02:37.283 --> 00:02:45.339
Each element carries a total weight c l, with c equal to minus rho A g, and sends half of it to each of its two nodes.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:51.232
Your turn: pause and do checkpoint six point one in your guide, and build the load vector.

00:02:54.465 --> 00:02:58.503
Node one gets c l over two from element one.

00:02:58.503 --> 00:03:06.397
Node two is shared: c l over two from element one, plus c l over two from element two.

00:03:06.397 --> 00:03:07.407
That's c l.

00:03:07.407 --> 00:03:09.701
Node three also gets c l.

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:13.465
Node four, at the top, only c l over two.

00:03:15.832 --> 00:03:23.512
And one more force: the ground pushes up on node one with a reaction R that we don't know yet.

00:03:23.512 --> 00:03:29.394
R is a force at node one like any other, so it goes in node one's entry.

00:03:29.394 --> 00:03:35.032
We include it so that row one stays a true equation we can use later.

00:03:35.365 --> 00:03:40.177
So K times q equals F, with four equations.

00:03:40.177 --> 00:03:43.086
But we can't solve it yet.

00:03:43.086 --> 00:03:45.325
Something's missing.

00:03:46.832 --> 00:03:52.821
Remember: every row of this matrix adds to zero, because sliding the whole column along stores no energy.

00:03:52.821 --> 00:03:58.182
So the bowl has a flat valley instead of a single lowest point, and the matrix has no inverse.

00:03:58.182 --> 00:04:02.232
Without its support, the column would just slide away under its weight.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:04.840
What's missing is the support.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:07.551
The base is fixed, so q one is zero.

00:04:07.551 --> 00:04:08.380
We know it.

00:04:08.661 --> 00:04:10.831
Here's what to do, and why.

00:04:10.831 --> 00:04:19.187
Because q one is zero, everything in column one gets multiplied by zero, so we can cross out column one.

00:04:19.187 --> 00:04:26.821
And row one is the equation that contains the unknown reaction R, so we set it aside for later.

00:04:29.121 --> 00:04:46.056
What's left is a three by three system for q two, q three and q four: E A over l, times two, minus one, zero; minus one, two, minus one; zero, minus one, one.

00:04:46.056 --> 00:04:51.201
On the right, c l over two, times two, two, one.

00:04:52.464 --> 00:04:58.578
To keep the numbers clean, measure every q in units of c l squared over two E A.

00:04:58.578 --> 00:05:08.664
Then the system is just: two, minus one, zero; minus one, two, minus one; zero, minus one, one, times the q's, equals two, two, one.

00:05:08.964 --> 00:05:16.044
The last row says minus x three plus x four equals one.

00:05:16.377 --> 00:05:21.937
Add the last row to the middle row: minus x two plus x three equals three.

00:05:22.277 --> 00:05:26.625
Add that result to the first row, and you get x two equals five.

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:29.477
Then x three is eight, and x four is nine.

00:05:29.811 --> 00:05:31.648
Now put the units back.

00:05:31.648 --> 00:05:41.708
c l squared over two E A, with c equal to minus rho A g and l equal to L over three, is minus rho g L squared over eighteen E.

00:05:41.708 --> 00:05:49.851
So the q's are rho g L squared over two E, times minus five ninths, minus eight ninths, and minus one.

00:05:50.311 --> 00:05:52.671
All negative: every point sinks.

00:05:52.671 --> 00:05:56.357
The top sinks most, by rho g L squared over two E.

00:05:56.357 --> 00:06:01.591
That answers our first question: that's how much the flagpole shortens.

00:06:02.631 --> 00:06:13.976
This column has an exact answer: u of x equals rho g over E, times x squared over two minus L x.

00:06:13.976 --> 00:06:15.631
Let's plot it.

00:06:15.931 --> 00:06:25.635
At x over L equal to one third, two thirds and one, it gives minus five ninths, minus eight ninths and minus one.

00:06:25.635 --> 00:06:28.211
The exact values at the nodes.

00:06:28.531 --> 00:06:34.851
Now the finite element answer: straight lines between those same node values.

00:06:35.164 --> 00:06:39.802
For a simple bar like this, linear elements put the nodes exactly right.

00:06:39.802 --> 00:06:43.088
In other structures the nodes are close, not exact.

00:06:43.088 --> 00:06:47.404
And between the nodes, straight lines can't quite follow the curve.

00:06:48.331 --> 00:06:49.130
Now stress.

00:06:49.130 --> 00:07:00.531
In each element, sigma is E times strain, and the strain is the change in displacement over the length: q of the top node minus q of the bottom node, over l.

00:07:01.441 --> 00:07:12.353
Element one: q two minus q one is minus five ninths, times rho g L squared over two E.

00:07:12.353 --> 00:07:20.601
Divide by L over three, multiply by E: minus five sixths rho g L.

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:25.941
Your turn: pause and do checkpoint six point two in your guide.

00:07:25.941 --> 00:07:28.421
Find the stress in element two.

00:07:31.654 --> 00:07:38.914
Element two: minus eight ninths plus five ninths is minus three ninths.

00:07:38.914 --> 00:07:42.697
The stress is minus one half rho g L.

00:07:42.697 --> 00:07:47.094
And element three, minus one sixth rho g L.

00:07:47.754 --> 00:07:48.633
Plot them.

00:07:48.633 --> 00:07:54.702
Each element's stress is a flat step, because its strain is constant.

00:07:54.702 --> 00:08:02.354
The exact stress is a straight line, from minus rho g L at the base to zero at the top.

00:08:02.754 --> 00:08:04.773
Look closely at where they meet.

00:08:04.773 --> 00:08:13.038
At x over L equal to one sixth, the middle of element one, the exact stress is minus five sixths rho g L: exactly the step's value.

00:08:13.038 --> 00:08:16.634
Each step crosses the line right at its element's middle.

00:08:17.021 --> 00:08:24.567
That answers our second question: the stress is highest at the base, where the column holds up its whole weight.

00:08:24.567 --> 00:08:31.642
And stresses are rougher than displacements, because they come from the slope of the straight-line guess.

00:08:31.642 --> 00:08:33.461
More elements, finer steps.

00:08:34.397 --> 00:08:37.446
Remember row one, the one we set aside?

00:08:37.446 --> 00:08:41.277
Now that we know q two, it tells us the reaction.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:49.524
It says E A over l, times q one minus q two, equals c l over two plus R.

00:08:49.524 --> 00:08:53.810
q one is zero, and q two we just found.

00:08:54.144 --> 00:08:58.456
Put the numbers in, and R comes out to rho g A L.

00:08:58.456 --> 00:09:03.736
The ground pushes up with exactly the column's whole weight.

00:09:03.736 --> 00:09:04.704
As it must.

00:09:05.110 --> 00:09:19.870
So, stepping back: assemble the blocks, add the loads, fix the supports by removing their rows and columns, solve, then get stresses and use the removed rows for the reactions.
